Haakon Studebaker <heptapod@yahoo.com> wrote in
news:Xns94E39E8965A5Aheptapodyahoocom@216.168.3.44: 

> I just wondered something when I stepped on a level teleport trap that
> took me to a level I never visited before during the game.
> How does my character know what level I am on after such an event?
> I'm sure if I was playing a gnome or dwarf that fact would be easily 
> ascertained assuming that Nethack dwarves and gnomes have stonecunning
> like that pen and paper game.

They probably just use a gnome-positioning-system.

> My idea is when a non-dwarf or non-gnome is teleported deeper into the
> dungeon or to a level that's been amnesiaed then they need to get
> their bearings to find out how deep they have been teleported.
> 
Not to mention that when you finally climb back up to the lowest partially 
mapped level you instantly know where the bit you already mapped is in 
relation to your current location.

So to do this idea properly, you want to not show already mapped but 
disjoint areas on a level, instead the previously mapped part should fill 
out only once you get back to where you have already been (lets assume you 
leave some magical spoor so you know where you've been). Also all levels 
should start mapping centred on the screen so you don't know how far they 
extend in each direction. Likewise disjoint levels wouldn't tell you which 
level they were. Enlightenment would, of course, reveal the current level.

